{# ── The Problem ── #}
<section>
  <div class="container" style="display:grid; gap:4rem; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; align-items:start;">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">The Problem</span>
      <h2>The January 31 deadline is earlier than most creators realize.</h2>
      <p>Form 1099-NEC (Nonemployee Compensation) must be sent to contractors and filed with the IRS by January 31 — the same date. That's weeks before the regular tax filing season even opens, and it catches creators off guard every year.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">Penalties for late or missing 1099s start at $60 per form and increase to $310 or higher per form for returns not filed by August 1 or cases of intentional disregard. For creators paying multiple contractors, that adds up fast.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">The other common mistake: not collecting W-9 information before making payments, then scrambling in January to track down contractors for their taxpayer IDs. Collecting a W-9 upfront — before any payment — is the right process, and I'll help you build that habit going forward.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4 style="margin-bottom:1rem;">1099 filing situations I handle for creators:</h4>
      <ul class="feature-list">
